# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# dns/dnsmasq.conf.example
# Phase 1 — Private Network Service Platform
#
# PURPOSE
# -------
# This is an EXAMPLE dnsmasq configuration for VM1 (the private
# DNS server).  Copy it to /etc/dnsmasq.conf (or drop it into
# /etc/dnsmasq.d/) on VM1 and adjust the IP if needed.
#
# WHY .test AND NOT .local
# -------------------------
# .local is reserved by mDNS/Avahi (RFC 6762) and causes
# conflicts on Linux/macOS networks.  We intentionally use the
# IANA-reserved .test TLD (RFC 2606) which is safe for private
# lab use and will never be registered on the public internet.
#
# HOW DNS IS STRUCTURED IN THIS PROJECT
# ---------------------------------------
# DNS does NOT point clients directly to the backend VMs.
# All names resolve to VM3 (Nginx), which acts as the single
# public entry point and reverse-proxies requests to the backends.
#
#   Client → DNS lookup → VM3 (Nginx :443)
#                              ↓
#                    Backend A or Backend B
#
# CURRENT LAB IP — VM3 (Nginx)
# -----------------------------
# The addresses below use the CURRENT lab IP of VM3: 192.168.64.6
# If the VM gets a new IP (DHCP / UTM network change), update
# BOTH address= lines and restart dnsmasq:
#   sudo systemctl restart dnsmasq
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Resolve app.teamX.test → VM3 Nginx (192.168.64.6) ────────
address=/app.teamX.test/192.168.64.6

# ── Resolve api.teamX.test → VM3 Nginx (192.168.64.6) ────────
address=/api.teamX.test/192.168.64.6

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL — Recommended dnsmasq hardening for a private lab
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Listen only on the internal interface (enp0s1 on VM1)
# interface=enp0s1

# Do not read /etc/resolv.conf (we are the authoritative server)
# no-resolv

# Forward unknown names to a public DNS (e.g. Google)
# server=8.8.8.8
# server=8.8.4.4

# Do not forward short, non-FQDN names to upstream
# domain-needed

# Do not forward names with no dot (guards against leaking local names)
# bogus-priv
